EXT. SALT FLAT - AFTERNOON

The ground is a white plate to every horizon, cracked into hexagons, and the sun sits on it like a coin on a table. A sedan lies tipped in a drainage cut a quarter mile back, one wheel still turning in the heat.

IMOGEN REYES-HALL, fifties, sunburnt, a silk scarf knotted over her hair, drags a plastic jug by its handle across the crust. Behind her, ABEL NKEMELU, twenty-six, limps with one shoe on and one shoe in his hand, his lips split and pale.

She stops. He stops. They look at the jug between them, the water in it barely above the lowest mark, and then at the horizon, which has not moved.

ABEL
How far d'you think?

IMOGEN
To the highway? Eleven miles. Maybe nine.

ABEL
You said six this morning.

IMOGEN
This morning I was optimistic.

ABEL
And now?

IMOGEN
Now I can count. We have a pint each. We can walk at night, when it's cold, and sleep through the worst of the day under the car. That's what the book says. That's what I'd tell my students to do.

ABEL
You teach survival?

IMOGEN
I teach art history. I read a book once.

ABEL
(laughing, then wincing)
God. Okay. Okay. Then why are we walking now?

IMOGEN
Because the car is a mile behind us and I could not make myself sit in it another hour. Because every minute I sat there I was listening for an engine.

ABEL
And?

IMOGEN
And there wasn't one. There was your breathing, and mine, and the metal ticking as it cooled. And I thought, if I stop moving I'll start counting, and if I start counting I'll know exactly how this ends.

ABEL
How does it end?

IMOGEN
You tell me. You're the one who wanted a shortcut.

ABEL
That's not fair.

IMOGEN
No. It isn't. I'm sorry.

ABEL
I saw the sign. I thought it said a gas station. It said a salt works. Closed. Since 1974.

IMOGEN
Abel. Stop.

ABEL
I'm just saying I read it wrong. Out loud. For the record. In case.

IMOGEN
In case what?

ABEL
In case one of us doesn't make it to the road, and the other one has to tell somebody.

She lifts the jug and shoves it into his hands. He holds it like something alive.

IMOGEN
Drink. Two mouthfuls. Then I drink two. Then we walk until the sun touches the ground, and then we walk some more, and when we reach the road you can tell them any story you like.

ABEL
(small, steady)
Two mouthfuls.

He drinks. Passes it. She drinks. Wipes her mouth with the back of her wrist and takes the jug, and they turn together toward the line where the white meets the sky.
